{"experiments":[
  {
    "hypothesis":"Tumor mutational burden (TMB) is positively associated with an immune checkpoint gene expression signature (PDCD1, CTLA4, LAG3) independent of ER status and age.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Test whether higher TMB correlates with higher expression of key immune checkpoint genes, adjusting for ER receptor status and patient age.",
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv and cleaned_clinical_data.csv. 2. From mutations, count per‐patient nonsynonymous mutations: filter Variant_Classification in {Missense_Mutation, Nonsense_Mutation, Frame_Shift_Del, Frame_Shift_Ins, Splice_Site, In_Frame_Del, In_Frame_Ins} and compute TMB as count per patient. 3. From gene expression, extract PDCD1, CTLA4, LAG3 expression values and compute an immune_checkpoint_score per patient as the average z‐score across these three genes. 4. Load clinical data and extract ER_status and age_at_diagnosis; encode ER_status as binary (Positive=1, else=0). 5. Merge TMB, immune_checkpoint_score, ER_status, and age_at_diagnosis by patient barcode. 6. Fit a multivariate linear regression: immune_checkpoint_score ~ TMB + ER_status + age_at_diagnosis. 7. Check model assumptions (residual normality, homoscedasticity). 8. Report regression coefficients, p‐values, and adjusted R². 9. Create scatter plot of immune_checkpoint_score vs TMB colored by ER_status with fitted regression line.",
      "deliverables":"1. Table of regression results (coefficients, SE, p‐values, adj. R²). 2. Diagnostic plots for regression assumptions. 3. Scatter plot with regression line showing the TMB–immune checkpoint relationship."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Triple-negative breast cancer patients (ER–, PR–, HER2–) have higher tumor mutational burden compared to other subtypes, controlling for stage and age.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Assess whether the TNBC subtype exhibits elevated TMB relative to non-TNBC subtypes when adjusting for pathologic stage and patient age.",
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_mutations.csv. 2. Define TNBC group: ER_status='Negative', PR_status='Negative', and her2_ihc_status='Negative' or her2_fish_status='Negative'. Label others Non-TNBC. 3. From mutations, compute each patient’s nonsynonymous mutation count as TMB. 4. From clinical data, extract pathologic_stage (simplify to I–II vs III–IV) and age_at_diagnosis. 5. Merge TMB, subtype label, stage group, and age. 6. Fit multivariate linear regression: TMB ~ TNBC_status + stage_group + age_at_diagnosis. 7. Validate regression assumptions. 8. Report coefficient for TNBC_status, p‐value, and model R². 9. Visualize boxplots of TMB by TNBC_status stratified by stage_group.",
      "deliverables":"1. Regression summary table. 2. Diagnostics for linear model. 3. Boxplots of TMB by subtype and stage."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"PIK3CA-mutated patients have improved overall survival compared to PIK3CA wild-type, after adjusting for age and pathologic stage.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Use multivariate Cox regression to determine whether PIK3CA mutations confer a survival advantage independent of age and tumor stage.",
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv and cleaned_clinical_data.csv. 2. Identify patients with any PIK3CA mutation (Hugo_Symbol='PIK3CA') and label them 'Mutant' vs 'WT'. 3. From clinical data, construct survival time and event: time = days_to_death if Dead else days_to_last_followup; event = (vital_status=='Dead'). 4. Extract age_at_diagnosis and pathologic_stage (extract roman numeral I–IV). 5. Merge PIK3CA status with survival and covariates by patient barcode. 6. Encode stage into categorical dummies and PIK3CA status as binary. 7. Fit Cox proportional hazards model: Surv(time,event) ~ PIK3CA_status + age_at_diagnosis + stage. 8. Test proportional hazards assumption. 9. Report hazard ratio and p‐value for PIK3CA_status, plus model concordance. 10. Plot adjusted survival curves for mutant vs wild-type.",
      "deliverables":"1. Cox model summary (HRs, 95% CI, p‐values, c‐index). 2. PH assumption test results. 3. Adjusted survival curves."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Black or African American patients have higher hazard of death compared to White patients after adjusting for age, stage, and tumor mutational burden.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Evaluate racial disparities in overall survival using multivariate Cox regression incorporating TMB as a biological covariate.",
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_mutations.csv. 2. Filter clinical data to Race in {'BLACK OR AFRICAN AMERICAN','WHITE'}. Encode race as binary (Black=1, White=0). 3. Compute per-patient TMB (# nonsynonymous mutations) from mutation data. 4. Construct survival time and event from clinical data. 5. Extract age_at_diagnosis and pathologic_stage (I–IV). 6. Merge race, TMB, survival, age, and stage. 7. Encode stage as dummy variables. 8. Fit Cox model: Surv(time,event) ~ race + age_at_diagnosis + stage + TMB. 9. Check PH assumption. 10. Report hazard ratio for race, p‐value, and model concordance. 11. Plot adjusted survival curves by race.",
      "deliverables":"1. Cox regression table with HR for race. 2. PH test summary. 3. Adjusted survival curves comparing races."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"High expression of the proliferation marker MKI67 is associated with shorter overall survival independently of age and stage.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Determine whether patients with high MKI67 tumor expression have worse survival controlling for standard clinical covariates.",
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_gene_expression.csv and cleaned_clinical_data.csv. 2. Extract MKI67 expression per patient; compute median and dichotomize patients into High vs Low expression. 3. From clinical data, derive survival time/event and extract age_at_diagnosis and pathologic_stage (I–IV). 4. Merge MKI67 group, survival, age, and stage. 5. Encode MKI67 group and stage as categorical dummies. 6. Fit Cox regression: Surv(time,event) ~ MKI67_high (1 vs 0) + age_at_diagnosis + stage. 7. Test proportional hazards assumption. 8. Report HR for MKI67_high, p‐value, and c‐index. 9. Plot adjusted Kaplan–Meier curves for MKI67 High vs Low.",
      "deliverables":"1. Cox summary with MKI67 effect. 2. PH assumption test. 3. Adjusted KM curves by MKI67 expression."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Positive surgical margin status increases risk of new tumor event after initial treatment, independent of stage and neoadjuvant therapy.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Assess the association between surgical margin status and subsequent new tumor events using logistic regression, adjusting for stage and neoadjuvant therapy.",
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv. 2. Select patients with non‐missing new_tumor_event and surgical_margin. Encode new_tumor_event as binary (YES=1, NO=0) and surgical_margin as Positive vs Negative (exclude 'Close'). 3. Extract pathologic_stage (I–IV) and neoadjuvant_treatment (Yes=1, No=0), plus age_at_diagnosis. 4. Encode stage as dummy variables. 5. Fit multivariate logistic regression: new_tumor_event ~ surgical_margin_positive + stage + neoadjuvant_treatment + age_at_diagnosis. 6. Check model fit (Hosmer–Lemeshow). 7. Report odds ratio for surgical_margin, p‐value, and model AUC. 8. Plot predicted probability of new tumor event by margin status.",
      "deliverables":"1. Logistic regression table with ORs and p‐values. 2. Model fit metrics (AUC, Hosmer–Lemeshow p‐value). 3. Bar plot of event rates by margin status."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"An epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT) gene signature score is higher in patients with lymph node involvement (N1+) versus N0, independent of tumor size (T stage).",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Test whether an EMT signature differentiates patients by nodal metastasis status, controlling for tumor size and age.",
      "steps":"1. Define EMT signature genes: CDH1, VIM, SNAI1, SNAI2, ZEB1. 2. Load cleaned_gene_expression.csv; extract expression for EMT genes; compute per-patient EMT_score as average z-score. 3. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv; simplify pathologic_n to N0 vs N1+ and pathologic_t to T1 vs T2+. Extract age_at_diagnosis. 4. Merge EMT_score with nodal_group, tumor_size_group, and age. 5. Fit multivariate linear regression: EMT_score ~ nodal_group + tumor_size_group + age_at_diagnosis. 6. Validate assumptions. 7. Report coefficient for nodal_group, p‐value, and R². 8. Create boxplots of EMT_score by nodal_group stratified by tumor_size_group.",
      "deliverables":"1. Regression summary. 2. Model diagnostics. 3. Stratified boxplots of EMT signature."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Receipt of postoperative pharmaceutical therapy reduces the hazard of new tumor events (disease-free survival), adjusting for stage and hormone receptor status.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Use Cox regression to determine whether postoperative pharmaceutical therapy improves disease-free survival (time to new tumor) independent of stage and ER/PR status.",
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv. 2. Select patients with non‐missing new_tumor_event and days_to_new_tumor. Construct DFS time = days_to_new_tumor and event = (new_tumor_event=='YES'). 3. Extract pharmaceutical_therapy (YES=1, NO=0), ER_status (Positive=1, else=0), PR_status, and pathologic_stage (I–IV). 4. Encode stage as dummy variables. 5. Fit Cox model: Surv(DFS_time, event) ~ pharmaceutical_therapy + ER_status + PR_status + stage. 6. Test PH assumption. 7. Report HR for pharmaceutical_therapy, p‐value, and c‐index. 8. Plot adjusted DFS curves for therapy vs no therapy.",
      "deliverables":"1. Cox model output (HR, CI, p‐values, c‐index). 2. PH test summary. 3. Adjusted disease-free survival curves."
    }
  }
]}
